ralph/progress.txt:7:- Customer-visible limits/usage reads now prefer current accepted subscription entitlements when one exists, joining them with `quota_usage` counters and billing-period reset windows; legacy `quota_policies` remain the fallback for organizations without a current subscription. Public projections expose source labels and thresholds but never plan ids, subscription ids, provider ids, override ids, or override reasons.
ralph/progress.txt:8:- Runtime entitlement resolution is now a first-class service in `internal/controlplane/entitlements`: build `entitlements.Resolver` over `store.SubscriptionRepository`, expose keyed snapshots with `value`/`enforcement_mode`/`source`/`explanation`, validate cache hits through `SubscriptionRepository.EntitlementRevision(org, at)`, and call `ResolveWithQuerier(ctx, tx, orgID, at)` from quota or worker units already inside `Store.Write` so entitlement reads share the same transaction snapshot as the guarded desired-state write. `quota.Checker` should be constructed with `quota.WithEntitlementResolver(resolver)` and keeps legacy `quota_policies` as fallback for dimensions with no entitlement key yet.
ralph/progress.txt:9:- Subscription entitlement resolution is a store-level deterministic layering rule: current subscriptions are `trialing|active|past_due` and inside `current_period_start <= at < current_period_end`; canceled/out-of-period subscriptions contribute no runtime entitlements; candidate precedence is plan defaults < `subscription_override` rows for that subscription < organization-level `emergency_admin` rows, with newest active `effective_from`, then `created_at`, then `id` as tie-breakers. Override reasons are redacted before persistence.
ralph/progress.txt:10:- Pricing plan catalog rows are versioned, global configuration rather than tenant data: `plans` uses immutable accepted versions keyed by `(slug, billing_period, version)` plus one active version per `(slug, billing_period)`, and `PricingPlanRepository.Update` archives the addressed row before inserting the next version and cloning entitlements. Do not update an accepted plan row in place.
ralph/progress.txt:259:- Quota enforcement: the QuotaRepository (internal/controlplane/store/quota.go) is the persistence half (EffectiveLimit / LockUsage / SumActiveReservations / InsertReservation, all tenant-scoped, mutations on *Tx) and the quota.Checker (internal/controlplane/quota) is the store.QuotaReserver service half. A hard limit is enforced by LockUsage doing INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING then SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on the quota_usage counter row — that lock serialises concurrent reservers so they cannot both over-allocate; the decision is current+reserved+requested <= limit, reserved = SUM of active non-expired reservations. Soft/metered limits record but never reject; disabled/no-policy allow with no write. A rejection is apierr.QuotaExceeded wrapping a recoverable quota.ExceededDetail{Resource,Current,Reserved,Requested,Limit}. Quota accounting rows have no domain.Kind — ids are minted locally with a prefixed crypto/rand helper.
ralph/progress.txt:2492:  plans table lands), resolves the effective limit, and for a hard limit locks
ralph/progress.txt:2513:    9 parallel reservations against a hard limit of 3 — exactly 3 commit).
ralph/progress.txt:21676:  1. TestServiceServiceCreateRejectsParallelOversubscriptionForDatabasesQuota — 20 goroutines race to create database-kind services against a hard limit of 5; services-dimension limit seeded LOOSE at 40 so the rejection's ExceededDetail.Resource can only attribute to "databases". Asserts exactly 5 successes, 15 rejections with CodeQuotaExceeded, detail.Resource=="databases", detail.Limit=5, detail.Requested=1, detail.Reserved==5 (lock serialised), detail.Current==0; redaction guard proves the rejection message never carries slug/request_id/correlation_id/service_id; counts services rows ==5 and quota_reservations rows ==5 for both databases AND services dimensions; SumActiveReservations(databases) ==5; authorizer.calls==20 and jobs.calls==5 (quota fails before enqueue).
